    "case_id": "560",
    "case_summary": "A 45-year-old African man presented with symptoms of palpitations, fatiguability, bipedal edema, weight loss, and diarrhea in the preceding weeks. He was a smoker with a 10 pack-year history. No other pertinent medical history.\n\n- Vital signs: Blood pressure 136/87 mm Hg (MAP 103 mm Hg)- Cardiovascular: Fast atrial fibrillation with ventricular rates of 170 beats/min- Lower extremities: Bipedal edema present- Neck: No obviously noted goiter- Cardiac auscultation: S1 with loud, palpable P2, pansystolic murmurs in tricuspid and mitral areas- Temperature: Afebrile\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Thyroid-stimulating hormone: <0.01 mIU/L- Free T4: 39.2 pmol/L- Thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor antibody: 39.33 IU/L- D-dimer: 5.48 mg/LImaging Studies:1. Electrocardiogram (Figure 1):   - Shows rapid atrial fibrillation   - Biventricular hypertrophy   - Right axis deviation2. Transthoracic and Transesophageal Echocardiography (Figure 2):   - Biventricular enlargement   - Left atrial dilatation (59 mm)   - Moderate mitral stenosis   - Severe mitral regurgitation   - Severe tricuspid regurgitation   - Left ventricular ejection fraction: 39%   - Pulmonary artery systolic pressure: 77 mm Hg   - Multiple intracardiac thrombi in:     * Left atrial appendage     * Posterior mitral leaflet     * Right ventricular outflow tract     * Proximal pulmonary artery3. CT Pulmonary Angiogram (Figure 3):   - Bilateral lower-lobe acute pulmonary emboli",
